From 392cc2c4431d7e467f6f10fb54fbc49320e92236 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 22:25:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][GBM] Dolby Vision rebuilt as HDR10 or SDR steps its
 fidelity down where the GPU cannot keep up

The measured fidelity step-down (decision 0030, CDVStepDown) existed for
native Dolby Vision output only. A small GPU that rebuilds a profile 7 FEL
video for a display without Dolby Vision and maps it to HDR10 or SDR missed
refreshes (an AMD Ryzen 3400GE mapping to 150 cd/m2: "Dropped 4, Skipped
2", reported by JonnyVee in the LibreELEC forum) with every picture drawn
at level 0.

The conversion now takes the level of each picture format by the same rule
(CDVConvertStep, DVConvertStep.h, over CDVStepDown), with what Kodi's
ordinary output can tell:

- the pace is the way that waits: a picture has one refresh period;
- its GPU time is that of the bridge's work on it (the rebuilding, the
  mapping and the copy into the window), timed by GL_EXT_disjoint_timer_query
  timers of its own, each with the picture it times;
- whether it missed its refresh comes from the flips: while pictures are
  rebuilt, the out-fence of every commit is read, and the commit that
  carried a new picture missed when its flip came more than one and a half
  periods after the flip before it; a flip not known tells nothing.

A level goes down only where both the GPU times and the missed refreshes
say so (or the GPU could not keep up in any case), to levels of the
picture's own ladder, and back up when the level above fits with room to
spare: a GPU that keeps up draws every picture at level 0, the same bytes as
before. The levels change filters, never the enhancement layer: a picture
whose layer is drawn has it drawn at every level. "Fidelity step-down"
(videoscreen.hadesdvstepmode) and advancedsettings.xml's <dvstepdown> decide
as for native output; what is found stays from one video to the next in a
session. kodi.log says how the settings are taken once, and each change of
level with the window that decided it.

The help of the setting says it applies to Dolby Vision shown as HDR10 or
SDR too (English, and es_es, es_ar, es_mx).
