 #: system/settings/settings.xml
 msgctxt "#59963"
-msgid "For experts. Where the graphics card cannot prepare a Dolby Vision picture in the time of a frame, even drawn the faster way and a frame ahead (typically an integrated one with 4K Blu-ray video that has an enhancement layer), the picture can be rebuilt slightly less precisely, with a simpler filter on some planes. In test pictures most changed pixels differ by the smallest step of a 12-bit picture. Automatic (the default): one step at a time and only as far as the video needs to play smoothly; graphics that keep up always show the exact picture, and what is found is kept for this card and build. Never: always the exact picture, even if the video stutters. Level 1, 2, 3: that level on every frame, to measure what it saves; nothing measured is kept meanwhile. A level set in advancedsettings.xml (dvstepdown) comes first. Takes effect from the next video."
+msgid "For experts. Where the graphics card cannot prepare a Dolby Vision picture in the time of a frame, even drawn the faster way and a frame ahead (typically an integrated one with 4K Blu-ray video that has an enhancement layer), the picture can be rebuilt slightly less precisely, with a simpler filter on some planes. In test pictures most changed pixels differ by the smallest step of a 12-bit picture. Automatic (the default): one step at a time and only as far as the video needs to play smoothly; graphics that keep up always show the exact picture, and what is found is kept for this card and build. Never: always the exact picture, even if the video stutters. Level 1, 2, 3: that level on every frame, to measure what it saves; nothing measured is kept meanwhile. A level set in advancedsettings.xml (dvstepdown) comes first. The same goes for Dolby Vision shown as HDR10 or SDR on a display without Dolby Vision, where the enhancement layer is still drawn at every level (what is found there is kept until Kodi stops). Takes effect from the next video."
